officials in afghanistan say thousands  of people are in need of clean water and  food following a devastating earthquake  more than a thousand people were killed  in the quake in a remote part of the  country near the pakistani border our  correspondent zaman rasheed joins us now  from the afghan capital car ball uh so  insane are the taliban government  beginning to sound desperate  well look jonathan i think it's starting  to look like a case of be careful what  you wish for yes the taliban government  here are in charge  but i think also they're slowly  realizing that in fact they are  dangerously desperate why is that and  that's because they've been isolated  from the western world with a nine  billion dollar asset freeze they are  facing the worst drought in four decades  they are going through a hunger crisis  and of course they've got a terrible  track record when it comes to human  rights rowing back on pledges they made  when they came into power last year a  sticking point for western governments  and now of course this catastrophic  earthquake are reminded to them that  they may not have the infrastructure in  place to deal with such a disaster  yesterday we spoke to someone from the  foreign ministry he said that they do  have the infrastructure and in fact that  it was the efforts of the coalition of  the last 20 years which has ruined a lot  of things for them and today we spoke to  another very senior member of the  government one who's actually been at  the epicenter of this earthquake and he  recalled what he saw  almost  um  okay  now that's something you don't see every  day whether you have sympathy for them  or not a member of this organization one  which is often tough powerful and and  often quite intimidating breaking down  like that but i think there's probably a  sign there that they need to do  something in order to change and to make  sure that if they want help in the  future they get it and also if they want  a seat at the international table they  will need to change and compromise
 

